"" {TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 10 "Problem 3." }{TEXT 
324 3 ".  " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
324 56 "There is a one-to-one correspondence between subsets of " }
{TEXT 229 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 324 28 " and binary lists of
 length " }{TEXT 230 1 "n" }{TEXT 324 17 ", that is, lists " }{TEXT 
231 3 "L =" }{TEXT 324 1 " " }{TEXT 232 22 "[x1, x2 , . . . , xn] " }
{TEXT 324 6 "where " }{TEXT 233 18 "x1, x2, . . . , xn" }{TEXT 324 25 
" are elements of the set " }{TEXT 234 5 "\{0,1\}" }{TEXT 324 57 ".  T
he correspondence is given by associating to the set " }{TEXT 235 1 "S
" }{TEXT 324 10 " the list " }{TEXT 236 1 "L" }{TEXT 324 7 " where " }
{TEXT 237 34 "xi = 1 if i is in S and 0 if not. " }{TEXT 324 78 "For e
xample, the set \{1,3,5\} corresponds to the list [1,0,1,0,1,0,0] if n
 = 7." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 1 "\n" }{TEXT 238 3 "(a)" }{TEXT 
324 19 " Write a procedure " }{TEXT 239 12 "list_to_set " }{TEXT 324 
79 "whose input is a binary list and whose output is the corresponding
 set. E. g., " }{TEXT 240 24 "list_to_set([1,0,1,0,1])" }{TEXT 324 21 
" will return the set " }{TEXT 241 7 "\{1,3,5\}" }{TEXT 324 44 ". Note
 that nops(L) is the length of a list." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 1 
"\n" }{TEXT 242 3 "(b)" }{TEXT 324 19 " Write a procedure " }{TEXT 
243 11 "set_to_list" }{TEXT 324 22 " whose input is a pair" }{TEXT 
244 4 " S,n" }{TEXT 324 7 " where " }{TEXT 245 1 "S" }{TEXT 324 16 " i
s a subset of " }{TEXT 246 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 324 5 " and
 " }{TEXT 247 1 "n" }{TEXT 324 69 " is a positive integer and whose ou
tput is the binary list of length " }{TEXT 248 1 "n" }{TEXT 324 26 " c
orresponding to the set " }{TEXT 249 1 "S" }{TEXT 324 23 ". E. g., if \+
n = 5 then " }{TEXT 250 22 "set_to_list(\{1,3,5\},5)" }{TEXT 324 13 " \+
will return " }{TEXT 251 11 "[1,0,1,0,1]" }{TEXT 324 2 ".\n" }{TEXT 
324 1 "\n" }{TEXT 252 3 "(c)" }{TEXT 324 59 " Show by a few tests that
 each procedure works. Then apply " }{TEXT 253 11 "set_to_list" }{TEXT
 324 32 " to each set in the powerset of " }{TEXT 254 12 "\{1, 2, 3, 4
\}" }{TEXT 324 158 " to form all binary lists of length 4. Make a prog
ram to print out a table of the following form. (But the order need no
t be the same as that started below.)\n" }{TEXT 324 1 "\n" }{TEXT 324 
24 "   [0,0,0,0] <-->  \{  \}\n" }{TEXT 324 24 "   [1,0,0 0] <--> \{ 1
 \}\n" }{TEXT 324 24 "   [0,1,0,0] <--> \{ 2 \} " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 324 12 "    ........" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 7 "    etc" }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 108 "Some e
xtra commas in the output is okay. You may obtain the power set of the
 set \{1,2,...,n\} by the command " }{TEXT 255 12 "powerset(n);" }
{TEXT 324 37 " but you must first load the package " }{TEXT 256 9 "com
binat." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 27 "list_to_set:=proc
(n::list)\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "local a, S, i;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "a:=
nops(n);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 7 "S:=\{\};\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "for i to a \+
do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 40 "if n[i]=1 then S:= S union \{i\}; end if;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "return S;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 
0 9 "end proc:" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 1 "\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "list_to_set([1,
0,1,0,1]);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "<%\"\"\"\"\"$\"\"&" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "restart:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 28 
"set_to_list:=proc(s::set,n)\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "local i, L;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "#a:=nops(s);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "#b:=max(s);\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "#L:=[];\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 22 "for i from 1 to n do \n" 
}{MPLTEXT 1 0 26 "  if i in s then L[i]:=1;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "  els
e L[i]:= 0; end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 27 "r
eturn [seq(L[i],i=1..n)];\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "end proc:" }{MPLTEXT 1 
0 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "set_to_list(\{1,5,
7\},10);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 4 "[r];" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 1 "\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 
16 "r:=[1,2,3,4,5];\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 11 "has([r],2);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 
"" {XPPMATH 20 "7,\"\"\"\"\"!F$F$F#F$F#F$F$F$" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "7#I\"rG6\"" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">I\"rG6\"7'\"
\"\"\"\"#\"\"$\"\"%\"\"&" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "I%trueG%*pro
tectedG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 257 11 "Problem  4." }{TEXT 
324 1 " " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
324 13 "Note that if " }{TEXT 258 1 "i" }{TEXT 324 20 " is an integer \+
then " }{TEXT 259 18 "convert(i,base, 2)" }{TEXT 324 14 " will convert
 " }{TEXT 260 2 "i " }{TEXT 324 47 "to a binary list (the binary repre
sentation of " }{TEXT 261 1 "i" }{TEXT 324 37 " in reverse order). The
n you may use " }{TEXT 262 11 "list_to_set" }{TEXT 324 46 " to convert
  this list to a set. Note that if " }{TEXT 263 1 "i" }{TEXT 324 11 " \+
runs from " }{TEXT 264 1 "0" }{TEXT 324 4 " to " }{XPPEDIT 205 0 "Type
setting:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"\", italic = \"true\", mathvariant = \+
\"italic\"), Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-msup(Typesetting:-mn(\"2\"
, font_style_name = \"CharacterStyle256\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), \+
Typesetting:-mi(\"n\", italic = \"true\", font_style_name = \"Characte
rStyle256\", mathvariant = \"italic\"), superscriptshift = \"0\"), Typ
esetting:-mo(\"&minus;\", font_style_name = \"CharacterStyle256\", mat
hvariant = \"normal\", fence = \"false\", separator = \"false\", stret
chy = \"false\", symmetric = \"false\", largeop = \"false\", movableli
mits = \"false\", accent = \"false\", lspace = \"0.2222222em\", rspace
 = \"0.2222222em\"), Typesetting:-mn(\"1\", font_style_name = \"Charac
terStyle256\", mathvariant = \"normal\")), Typesetting:-mi(\"\", itali
c = \"true\", mathvariant = \"italic\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6
\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6%-I#miGF$6%Q!F'/%'italicGQ%trueF'/%,mathv
ariantGQ'italicF'-F#6%-I%msupGF$6%-I#mnGF$6%Q\"2F'/%0font_style_nameGQ
2CharacterStyle256F'/F3Q'normalF'-F,6&Q\"nF'F/F>F2/%1superscriptshiftG
Q\"0F'-I#moGF$6.Q(&minus;F'F>FA/%&fenceGQ&falseF'/%*separatorGFO/%)str
etchyGFO/%*symmetricGFO/%(largeopGFO/%.movablelimitsGFO/%'accentGFO/%'
lspaceGQ,0.2222222emF'/%'rspaceGFhn-F;6%Q\"1F'F>FAF+" }{TEXT 324 6 " t
hen " }{TEXT 265 32 "list_to_set(convert(i,base,2) ) " }{TEXT 324 33 "
 will run through all subsets of " }{TEXT 266 16 "\{1, 2, . . ., n\}" 
}{TEXT 324 3 ". \n" }{TEXT 324 1 "\n" }{TEXT 267 3 "(a)" }{TEXT 324 
42 " Use this idea to make your own procedure " }{TEXT 268 9 "PowerSet
 " }{TEXT 324 17 "which will given " }{TEXT 269 1 "n" }{TEXT 324 27 ",
  produce the powerset of " }{TEXT 270 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT
 324 87 ". Show by several examples that your procedure works. For you
r examples you should get " }{TEXT 271 24 "nops(PowerSet(n)) = 2^(n" }
{TEXT 324 32 "). Check that this is the case.\n" }{TEXT 324 2 " \n" }
{TEXT 272 3 "(b)" }{TEXT 324 35 " Use this idea to make a procedure " 
}{TEXT 273 7 "RandSet" }{TEXT 324 43 " which given n produces a random
 subset of " }{TEXT 274 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 324 7 ". [Use 
" }{TEXT 275 4 "rand" }{TEXT 324 42 " to produce a random integer in t
he range " }{TEXT 276 8 "0..2^n-1" }{TEXT 324 36 " and then convert it
 to a subset of " }{TEXT 277 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 324 1 "."
 }{TEXT 278 3 "]  " }{TEXT 279 31 "Do NOT use PowerSet or powerset" }
{TEXT 324 240 ". Show by examples that it works for small n such as 5,
 10, and 20 as well as for large n such as 100. It will even work for \+
n = 1000, but the output will be rather large. On average a random sub
set of \{1,2,...,n\} will contain n/2 elements." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 280 7 "[Remark" }{TEXT 324 62 ": The powerset of a set with 1000
 elements contains more than " }{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typesetting:-mrow(Types
etting:-mi(\"\", italic = \"true\", mathvariant = \"italic\"), Typeset
ting:-mrow(Typesetting:-msup(Typesetting:-mn(\"10\", executable = \"fa
lse\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), Typesetting:-mn(\"300\", executable \+
= \"false\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), superscriptshift = \"0\")), Ty
pesetting:-mi(\"\", italic = \"true\", mathvariant = \"italic\"));" "-
I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6%-I#miGF$6%Q!F'/%
'italicGQ%trueF'/%,mathvariantGQ'italicF'-F#6#-I%msupGF$6%-I#mnGF$6%Q#
10F'/%+executableGQ&falseF'/F3Q'normalF'-F;6%Q$300F'F>FA/%1superscript
shiftGQ\"0F'F+" }{TEXT 324 171 " elements. This is, according to curre
nt estimates, more than the number of atoms in the whole universe. So \+
you cannot expect your computer to be able to store such a set." }
{TEXT 281 1 "]" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 205 10 "Problem 5." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 105 "Construct an original \+
example of each of the following types of Maple expressions. Check eac
h using both " }{TEXT 282 4 "type" }{TEXT 324 5 " and " }{TEXT 283 10 
"whattype. " }{TEXT 324 25 "Note that in the case of " }{TEXT 284 8 "w
hattype" }{TEXT 324 21 " you may need to use " }{TEXT 285 4 "eval" }
{TEXT 324 151 " as illustrated in the lecture. To separate the example
s make a line using dashes like the following (in text mode) between t
he examples for each type." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 70 "----------
------------------------------------------------------------" }}{PARA 
0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 205 5 "list\n" }{TEXT
 205 3 "set" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 205 6 "array\n" }{TEXT 205 7 "mat
rix\n" }{TEXT 205 6 "Matrix" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 205 6 "Vector" }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 205 6 "table\n" }{TEXT 205 8 "integer\n" }{TEXT 
205 9 "procedure" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 205 8 "function" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 55 "L:=[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9]; whattype(L)
; whattype(eval(L));" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">I\"LG6\"7+\"\"
\"\"\"#\"\"$\"\"%\"\"&\"\"'\"\"(\"\")\"\"*" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
