{TEXT 205 11 "Problem 5. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 
"" 0 "" {TEXT 324 256 "Show that if you increase the upper bound for n
 in Problem 4  from 20 to a higher value you will not obtain any addit
ional happy numbers less than 320. This also implies that the previous
ly computed 50 happy numbers are the first 50 happy numbers, indeed. "
 }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 1 " " }
{TEXT 322 5 "Hints" }{TEXT 324 31 ": Show that if in the sequence " }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 1 "\n" }{TEXT 324 51 "             n, f(n), \+
(f@@2)(n), . . ., (f@@20)(n)\n" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 205 249 "a 4 i
s reached before a 1 is reached then a 1 will never be reached, so the
 number n will not be happy. What happens to the sequence once 4 is re
ached? Look at some examples. Then show that in 20 iterations a 1 or a
 4 is reached for n less than 320." }{TEXT 205 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 
"> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}}
